[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Web Components: Creating Reusable Custom HTML Elements"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Web Components are a set of web platform APIs that allow you to create reusable custom HTML elements with encapsulated functionality. They enable you to extend HTML vocabulary, making your code more modular, maintainable, and readable. This tutorial will guide you through the process of creating your own web components."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Concepts of Web Components"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Custom Elements:**  Allow you to define your own HTML tags and their behavior."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Shadow DOM:** Provides encapsulation by creating a separate DOM tree for the component, shielding it from external styles and scripts."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**HTML Templates:**  Reusable HTML snippets that can be cloned and inserted into the DOM."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**HTML Imports (Deprecated):** A way to import HTML documents containing web component definitions.  While deprecated, understanding the concept is useful for historical context.  Modern approaches use JavaScript modules."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating a Simple Custom Element"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Let's start by creating a simple custom element called `<my-element>`. This element will simply display a greeting message."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"class MyElement extends HTMLElement {
  constructor() {
    super();
    this.innerHTML = '<h1>Hello from my-element!</h1>';
  }
}

customElements.define('my-element', MyElement);"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Explanation:"},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"We define a class `MyElement` that extends `HTMLElement`. This is the base class for all custom elements."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The `constructor()` is called when the element is created.  We call `super()` to invoke the parent class's constructor."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"We set the `innerHTML` of the element to display the greeting message."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`customElements.define('my-element', MyElement)` registers the custom element with the browser. The first argument is the tag name (must contain a hyphen), and the second is the class that defines the element's behavior."},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"To use the custom element in your HTML, simply add the tag to your HTML file:"},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"<my-element></my-element>"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Using Shadow DOM for Encapsulation"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"Shadow DOM provides encapsulation, preventing styles and scripts from outside the component from affecting its internal structure. Let's modify our example to use Shadow DOM."},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"class MyElement extends HTMLElement {
  constructor() {
    super();
    const shadow = this.attachShadow({ mode: 'open' });
    shadow.innerHTML = '<h1>Hello from my-element (Shadow DOM)!</h1>';
  }
}

customElements.define('my-element', MyElement);"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"Explanation:"},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`this.attachShadow({ mode: 'open' })` creates a shadow DOM and attaches it to the element. The `mode` option can be 'open' (accessible from JavaScript outside the component) or 'closed' (not accessible)."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Instead of setting `this.innerHTML`, we set `shadow.innerHTML` to add content to the shadow DOM."},{"order":24,"type":"subheading","text":"Adding Styles to a Web Component"},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can add styles to your web component either inline or by using a `<style>` tag within the shadow DOM.  Let's add some styling."},{"order":26,"type":"code","text":"class MyElement extends HTMLElement {
  constructor() {
    super();
    const shadow = this.attachShadow({ mode: 'open' });
    const h1 = document.createElement('h1');
    h1.textContent = 'Hello from my-element (Shadow DOM)!';
    const style = document.createElement('style');
    style.textContent = `
      h1 {
        color: blue;
        text-align: center;
      }
    `;
    shadow.appendChild(style);
    shadow.appendChild(h1);
  }
}

customElements.define('my-element', MyElement);"},{"order":27,"type":"paragraph","text":"Explanation:"},{"order":28,"type":"bullet_point","text":"We create a `<style>` element and set its `textContent` to our CSS rules."},{"order":29,"type":"bullet_point","text":"We append the `<style>` element and the `<h1>` element to the shadow DOM."},{"order":30,"type":"subheading","text":"Using Templates"},{"order":31,"type":"paragraph","text":"HTML templates allow you to define reusable HTML structures. This is especially useful for complex web components.  Let's create a template for our element."},{"order":32,"type":"code","text":"<template id="my-element-template">
  <style>
    h1 {
      color: blue;
      text-align: center;
    }
  </style>
  <h1>Hello from my-element (Shadow DOM with Template)!</h1>
</template>

<script>
  class MyElement extends HTMLElement {
    constructor() {
      super();
      const shadow = this.attachShadow({ mode: 'open' });
      const template = document.getElementById('my-element-template');
      const content = template.content.cloneNode(true);
      shadow.appendChild(content);
    }
  }

  customElements.define('my-element', MyElement);
</script>"},{"order":33,"type":"paragraph","text":"Explanation:"},{"order":34,"type":"bullet_point","text":"We define a `<template>` element with an `id`."},{"order":35,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Inside the template, we define the HTML structure and styles for our component."},{"order":36,"type":"bullet_point","text":"In the component's constructor, we get the template element using `document.getElementById()`."},{"order":37,"type":"bullet_point","text":"We clone the template's content using `template.content.cloneNode(true)` (the `true` argument ensures that all child nodes are also cloned)."},{"order":38,"type":"bullet_point","text":"We append the cloned content to the shadow DOM."},{"order":39,"type":"subheading","text":"Attributes and Properties"},{"order":40,"type":"paragraph","text":"Web components can have attributes that can be set from HTML and properties that can be accessed and modified from JavaScript. Let's add an attribute to our element to customize the greeting message."},{"order":41,"type":"code","text":"class MyElement extends HTMLElement {
  constructor() {
    super();
    this.attachShadow({ mode: 'open' });
  }

  static get observedAttributes() {
    return ['message'];
  }

  attributeChangedCallback(name, oldValue, newValue) {
    if (name === 'message') {
      this.shadowRoot.innerHTML = `<h1>${newValue}</h1>`;
    }
  }
}

customElements.define('my-element', MyElement);"},{"order":42,"type":"paragraph","text":"Explanation:"},{"order":43,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`static get observedAttributes()` returns an array of attribute names that the component is interested in. When these attributes change, the `attributeChangedCallback` is called."},{"order":44,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`attributeChangedCallback(name, oldValue, newValue)` is called when an observed attribute changes. The `name` argument is the name of the attribute, `oldValue` is the previous value, and `newValue` is the new value."},{"order":45,"type":"bullet_point","text":"We update the shadow DOM's content with the new message."},{"order":46,"type":"paragraph","text":"Now you can use the `message` attribute in your HTML:"},{"order":47,"type":"code","text":"<my-element message="Hello, World!"></my-element>
<my-element message="Greetings!"></my-element>"},{"order":48,"type":"subheading","text":"Events"},{"order":49,"type":"paragraph","text":"Web components can dispatch custom events to communicate with the outside world. Let's add a button to our element that dispatches an event when clicked."},{"order":50,"type":"code","text":"class MyElement extends HTMLElement {
  constructor() {
    super();
    const shadow = this.attachShadow({ mode: 'open' });
    const button = document.createElement('button');
    button.textContent = 'Click Me!';
    button.addEventListener('click', () => {
      const event = new CustomEvent('my-event', { detail: { message: 'Button clicked!' } });
      this.dispatchEvent(event);
    });
    shadow.appendChild(button);
  }
}

customElements.define('my-element', MyElement);"},{"order":51,"type":"paragraph","text":"Explanation:"},{"order":52,"type":"bullet_point","text":"We create a `<button>` element and add a click event listener."},{"order":53,"type":"bullet_point","text":"When the button is clicked, we create a `CustomEvent` called 'my-event'. The `detail` property can contain any data you want to pass with the event."},{"order":54,"type":"bullet_point","text":"We dispatch the event using `this.dispatchEvent(event)`."},{"order":55,"type":"paragraph","text":"To listen for the event, you can add an event listener to the custom element:"},{"order":56,"type":"code","text":"const myElement = document.querySelector('my-element');
myElement.addEventListener('my-event', (event) => {
  console.log(event.detail.message);
});"},{"order":57,"type":"subheading","text":"Lifecycle Callbacks"},{"order":58,"type":"paragraph","text":"Web components have several lifecycle callbacks that are called at different stages of the element's life:"},{"order":59,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**connectedCallback():** Called when the element is connected to the DOM."},{"order":60,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**disconnectedCallback():** Called when the element is disconnected from the DOM."},{"order":61,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**adoptedCallback():** Called when the element is moved to a new document."},{"order":62,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**attributeChangedCallback(name, oldValue, newValue):** Called when an observed attribute changes (as shown above)."},{"order":63,"type":"paragraph","text":"These callbacks allow you to perform setup and cleanup tasks when the element is added to or removed from the DOM."},{"order":64,"type":"subheading","text":"JavaScript Modules"},{"order":65,"type":"paragraph","text":"For larger projects, it's best to organize your web component code into JavaScript modules. This allows you to import and reuse components across your application."},{"order":66,"type":"paragraph","text":"Create a file named `my-element.js` with the following content:"},{"order":67,"type":"code","text":"export class MyElement extends HTMLElement {
  constructor() {
    super();
    this.attachShadow({ mode: 'open' });
    this.shadowRoot.innerHTML = '<h1>Hello from my-element (Module)!</h1>';
  }
}

customElements.define('my-element', MyElement);"},{"order":68,"type":"paragraph","text":"Then, in your HTML file, import the module:"},{"order":69,"type":"code","text":"<script type="module" src="my-element.js"></script>
<my-element></my-element>"},{"order":70,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `type="module"` attribute tells the browser to treat the script as a JavaScript module."},{"order":71,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":72,"type":"paragraph","text":"Web Components provide a powerful way to create reusable, encapsulated HTML elements. By using custom elements, shadow DOM, HTML templates, and JavaScript modules, you can build complex and maintainable web applications. This tutorial has provided a basic introduction to web components. Explore the Web Component specifications for more advanced features and techniques."}]
